{
  "dom_id": "step-e1458",
  "name": "Example: Further Identifying True Statements Regarding Discriminants",
  "problem": "The graph of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ (shown below) has discriminant $\\mathcal D.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathcal D < 0$\n\nII. $a > 0$\n\nIII. $c < 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266874/assets/68d51d31798531b7516af556d4bdf2d1b3d5f9be904836554785cf1ff9bc69c4.png)",
  "worked_solution": "For the given graph of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c{:}$\n\n- Notice that the parabola does not intersect the $x$-axis, so it has no roots. Therefore, we have $\\mathcal{D} \\lt 0,$ and hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The value $a$ is the leading coefficient. Since the parabola opens downward, we have that $a \\lt 0.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- The value $c$ represents the $y$-intercept of the parabola. The $y$-intercept is below the $x$-axis, so it must be negative. Therefore, we must have $c < 0,$ and hence, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
